WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.509
Let's go next to our caller from the eight
one six area code.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:09.020
Who's calling today from eight one six?

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.020
A David.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.790
This is Jazz from Kansas City, Missouri.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:14.330
Hey, Jazz, what's going on?

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:21.160
So I was calling to ask you about how you're
voting in November.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.160
OK.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.500
Specifically on a ballot measure.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.539
It looks like Massachusetts will probably
have ranked choice voting on the ballot.

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:31.560
Will you be voting for it?

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.301
I've not seen the language of it.

00:00:33.301 --> 00:00:34.301
I actually didn't.

00:00:34.301 --> 00:00:38.010
I need to get schooled up on what's on the
ballot in November.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.450
So the answer is depending on how it is written.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:48.060
I very well may and I. I've done a lot of
interviews in segments on a number of different

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.120
voting systems.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.540
There's the sort of standard configuration
of ranked choice voting.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.219
There is single transferable vote.

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:58.710
There's a bunch of different configurations.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.220
And I like a lot of them.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.000
Some of them are less than perfectly structured.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.489
And so I need to look at how it's written.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.000
But that is certainly something I would consider
voting for until I actually see the text of

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.000
the initiative.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.570
I wouldn't want to say for sure.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:19.850
Actually, Maine has instant runoff voting,
and I'm pretty sure that's what Massachusetts

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.670
is proposing.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:29.350
And it's the it's it seems like the easiest
to implement system within it given our current

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.350
constitutional structure.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.350
But, yeah.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:35.140
That's another things that are Asian, which
is sometimes the best system, is more complex

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.229
constitutionally to implement.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:41.740
And you also don't want to let the perfect
become the enemy of the good at someone.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.520
Someone once said, I don't know who.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:49.450
Yeah, yeah, well, first past the post is arguably
the worst.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:50.450
Right.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:58.240
But do you think progressives should focus
on electoral reform, like on ballot initiatives

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.090
such as the main one and the Massachusetts
one?

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.090
Absolutely.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.090
So what for?

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:09.809
For several years now, one of the frame the
framework I've been prioritizing is we need

00:02:09.809 --> 00:02:12.189
to fix how politics is financed.

00:02:12.189 --> 00:02:15.230
And we need to fix how we vote.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.930
And this can include getting rid of first
past the post.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.930
This can include getting away from the Electoral
College system.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:27.230
So, yeah, it's like if you care about education
or abortion or climate or whatever, to really

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.879
make progress on these issues, you've got
to fix some of the structural problems that

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:31.879
we have.

00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:35.049
Health care, I would consider one of those
structural problems in the sense that if you

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:39.870
are stuck at a job merely to avoid losing
health insurance, it's going to be difficult

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.920
to be an activist for what you care about.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.120
So absolutely, this should be a priority for
progressives.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.890
OK.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.519
And one last question, if you don't mind.

00:02:50.519 --> 00:02:51.519
Sure.

00:02:51.519 --> 00:02:56.790
Since Maine has ranked choice voting now and
they're going to be voting in 2020, rank choice

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.790
voting.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.279
The instant runoff.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:02.779
How would you feel about Progressive's in
Maine putting Lisa Savage, the Green Party

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:06.150
candidate, as their number one in the race
against Susan Collins?

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.030
I know nothing about her, so I would have
to know.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.939
I mean, I get the question is more of a mathematical
question.

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:16.699
You can vote for the Green Party candidate
without helping Susan Collins.

00:03:16.699 --> 00:03:20.370
If you have a system other than first past
the post.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.569
But but even still, given that I know nothing
about her, it wouldn't be appropriate for

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:26.489
me to say, yes, do it, because who knows?

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:28.340
I just don't know enough about her.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:29.340
Right.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.340
Right.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:35.730
I was more so just asking generally if under
instant runoff system, you think that voting

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:39.280
Green Party as number one would be a good
idea?

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.219
Only if you support the Green Party.

00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:47.019
What the statement that I can say more generally
is, with such a system, you can vote for the

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:52.889
person you most agree with without fear that
you may be helping the person you're most

00:03:52.889 --> 00:03:53.889
opposed to.

00:03:53.889 --> 00:03:55.989
That would be how I would generalize the rule.

00:03:55.989 --> 00:03:57.169
All right.

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:58.169
Yeah.

00:03:58.169 --> 00:03:59.169
Cool.

00:03:59.169 --> 00:04:00.169
Sounds good.

00:04:00.169 --> 00:04:01.489
All right, my friend.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:02.731
Thank you very much for the phone call.

00:04:02.731 --> 00:04:03.731
Appreciate hearing from you.

00:04:03.731 --> 00:04:04.731
Let's go to let's go to our friend Don Tell
from Florida.

00:04:04.731 --> 00:04:05.731
Don, tell r how are things going down there?

00:04:05.731 --> 00:04:06.731
What would the virus exploding?

00:04:06.731 --> 00:04:07.731
Don Taylor, are we muted, we can't hear you.

00:04:07.731 --> 00:04:08.731
All right, Don, tell we're going to one more
opportunity I wanted to hear from you, Don

00:04:08.731 --> 00:04:09.731
Talbot.

00:04:09.731 --> 00:04:10.731
We're not you're not coming through at all.

00:04:10.731 --> 00:04:11.731
All right.

00:04:11.731 --> 00:04:11.743
No, Don, tell that's too bad, was wanted to
hear from him.

